# 4.4 Deploy and trace

1. Deploy from `wanda-agent/`:

    ```bash
    agentbricks deploy --no-wait
    agentbricks deployments wait {{ n.app }}
    ```

    A first deploy takes a few minutes, mostly to start the app's compute. If `wait` says it is still deploying, run it again. Deploy creates the memory and session stores, wires in tracing, and configures the agent to act as the signed-in user for the UC function tools.

2. Ask the deployed agent a question:

    ```bash
    agentbricks endpoint invoke {{ n.app }} --path /api/invocations \
      --json "{\"id\":\"$(uuidgen)\",\"session_id\":\"$(uuidgen)\",\"input\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"What is the status of booking 532?\"}]}"
    ```

3. Verify the response has `"status": "completed"` and says booking 532 is confirmed.
4. Open **Apps** in the workspace sidebar, click `{{ n.app }}` and open its URL to chat in the browser.
5. Find the deployed agent's traces in the SQL editor:

    ```sql
    SELECT trace_id, min(from_unixtime(start_time_unix_nano / 1e9)) AS started, collect_set(name) AS spans
    FROM <catalog>.{{ n.trace_schema }}.wanda_otel_spans
    GROUP BY trace_id ORDER BY started DESC LIMIT 5;
    ```

6. Verify the newest traces include an `invoke` span and spans named after the UC functions, for example `<catalog>__{{ n.schema }}__get_booking`.

!!! tip "If a deploy fails"
    Run `agentbricks deployments logs {{ n.app }}`. A failed first deploy keeps the app; run `agentbricks deploy` again to resume.

Wanda now runs on Databricks, reads governed data as the user, asks before it refunds, and traces every run to Unity Catalog. Next you prove it gives the right answers.

